{"name":"Sea Drifter","hex":"#4b7794","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"},{"term":"drifter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who moves from place to place or job to job.","A type of lightweight sail used in light winds like a spinnaker."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who takes part in drift fishing.","A boat used for drift fishing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drifter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drifter (oceanography)","blocks":[{"text":"A drifter is an oceanographic device floating on the surface to investigate ocean currents by tracking location. They can also measure other parameters like sea surface temperature, salinity, barometric pressure, and wave height. Modern drifters are typically tracked by satellite, often GPS. They are sometimes called Lagrangian drifters since the location of the measurements they make moves with the flow. A major user of drifters is NOAA's Global Drifter Program."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/SVP-B.jpg/330px-SVP-B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drifter_(oceanography)","wikidata":"Q2451097"},{"title":"Drifter (fishing boat)","blocks":[{"text":"A drifter is a type of fishing boat. They were designed to catch herring in a long drift net. Herring fishing using drifters has a long history in the Netherlands and in many British fishing ports, particularly in East Scottish ports."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Steam_Drifter_Lydia_Eva.jpg/330px-Steam_Drifter_Lydia_Eva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drifter_(fishing_boat)","wikidata":"Q4168239"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/20130516DrifterBuoysNOAA.jpg/500px-20130516DrifterBuoysNOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/20130516DrifterBuoysNOAA.jpg/500px-20130516DrifterBuoysNOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A drifter neatly compressed for deployment (left) and with the nylon drogue fully extended (right).[18]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20130516DrifterBuoysNOAA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Rigging2.png/330px-Rigging2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Rigging2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Rigging2.png/330px-Rigging2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARigging2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Groenewegen.D3.Buis.jpg/250px-Groenewegen.D3.Buis.jp